E
"tapestry tapestries walls"
"Although faded and frayed, the tapestry was once a work of art sewn of

the finest of silks.  No one knows how the Wayfarer's came by this

treasure, for when asked, the proprietor just shrugs and says, \"It was

always here.\" 


The tapestry, its background a deep midnight blue, depicts a panoramic

view of a sprawling city divided in twain by a lazy blue-green river. 

The scene, stitched in finest silks, portrays a slight woman standing in

front of a wide portcullis, a massive grey-stone keep looming behind her. 

The foreground of the picture, sewn in increasingly more detail, reveals

an island teeming with life.  Centrally, shabby stalls and multi-colored

tents surround a vast green field; temporary structures erected for what

is obviously a fair or festival.  Dancing bears, a handful of dogs

dressed up as clowns, a roped-off area where two men wrestle while a

crowd of bystanders cheers them on, people strolling arm in arm or

singly...these all make up the middle ground of the scene.  At the very

front of the needlework, against a backdrop of majestic oaks, lies a

jousting field, two combatants posturing off to either end adjacent to

the spectator-filled stands.  The work is masterfully crafted, although

old, signs of wear and frayed threads suggesting its age.  Worked in gold

thread, at the bottom right corner of the piece, is a signature and date:

Lady Margalis Stormblade, 21...the rest of the numbers are worn away.

"
E
"fireplace fire hearth"
"Crafted of smooth, round river rock, a huge fireplace fills one wall of the

tavern.  Within it's cavernous maw, a flickering fire of still-damp wood

burns, cracking and spitting.  Occasionally, when the wind is strong or

changes directions suddenly, a puff of smoke belches from the chimney and

rises to the ceiling, hanging beneath the rafters like a pale gray shroud.

"
E
